{
  "scenario_id": "sc-3c83fd",
  "overall_assessment": "anomalies",
  "findings": [
    {
      "benefit": "CI",
      "years": [2016, 2024],
      "pattern": "volatility",
      "direction": "dispersion",
      "magnitude": "Yearly CI A/E shows much larger scatter than sampling noise: sd(AE) approx 0.13 vs expected sampling sd approx 0.02-0.04; swings from 0.731 (2019) to 1.100 (2021). Unweighted mean AE approx 1.00 and overall weighted AE only 1.014, so no clear central level shift.",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "evidence": [
        "artifacts/ae_ci_by_year.csv: 2016 AE=1.098, 2017 AE=0.927, 2018 AE=0.808, 2019 AE=0.731, 2020 AE=1.067, 2021 AE=1.100, 2022 AE=1.084, 2023 AE=1.090, 2024 AE=1.055",
        "The offsetting high and low years keep the overall CI A/E close to 1 (summary.json overall CI A/E=1.0143), which is not enough to explain the large year-to-year dispersion",
        "With expected claim counts around 600-1900, binomial/Poisson A/E noise should be roughly 0.02-0.04, while several yearly deviations exceed 0.10-0.27"
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Investigate CI claims data and reporting for 2016-2024 first: check for missing/lagged claim payments, definition or coding changes, and data-feed issues. If the data are valid, treat this as increased experience dispersion rather than a change in expected claim levels: review CI volatility/process variance assumptions, valuation risk margins, and any pricing loadings."
    }
  ]
}

